"use strict";(self.webpackChunkmy_blog=self.webpackChunkmy_blog||[]).push([["4759"],{64412(e,t,a){a.r(t),a.d(t,{assets:()=>l,contentTitle:()=>r,default:()=>u,frontMatter:()=>s,metadata:()=>o,toc:()=>h});var o=a(35481),i=a(74848),n=a(28453);let s={slug:"ambitious-with-ai",title:"\u5982\u4F55\u66F4\u6709\u96C4\u5FC3\u5730\u4F7F\u7528 AI",date:new Date("2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z")},r,l={authorsImageUrls:[]},h=[{value:"Altitude",id:"altitude",level:2},{value:"Depth",id:"depth",level:2},{value:"Range",id:"range",level:2},{value:"We have been here before",id:"we-have-been-here-before",level:2}];function d(e){let t={h2:"h2",p:"p",...(0,n.R)(),...e.components};return(0,i.jsxs)(i.Fragment,{children:["\n","\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"I work on Claude. It\u2019s an interesting experience: Every day I learn about yet another feat accomplished with or by Claude that I hadn\u2019t considered, didn\u2019t think possible, or simply lacked the curiosity and time to try. This month, it may have helped discover a new planet, I\u2019ve seen the first AI music video that I really like, and we are discussing a new era of math."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Model capabilities don\u2019t grow uniformly. Models are, as Terence Tao puts it, \u201Castounding in some directions, while inadequate in others\u201D. Some people manage to pilot the astounding capabilities and seem to be flying \u2014 it\u2019s addicting to watch people reach incredible heights with the products I\u2019ve built. Many others are still struggling to get their new wings to work, wondering what they\u2019re good for beyond asking \u201CWhat is the capital of France\u201D or \u201CCan you make this email sound less angry\u201D."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"I frequently proclaim (together with other people working on AI) that we ought to be more ambitious when using it. It\u2019s become a rallying cry. Whenever we see something impressive, we repost and write \u201Cwe\u2019re not ambitious enough\u201D. I understand how tricky it is to follow that advice, so here\u2019s a tutorial on being more ambitious."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"I think of ambition in four types: Speed, Altitude, Depth, and Range."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Speed: The same work in less time, the bread and butter for AI\u2019s economic value: If you need some code written, I\u2019ll do it faster with Claude. Well established and requires little creativity in how you approach AI, so I\u2019ll skip this one.\nAltitude: Going up in abstraction and considering the outcome rather than the individual task.\nDepth: Considering a task the way a field-leading expert would. Rather than operating at the task\u2019s layer, we go one level deeper and touch the underlying data, tools, and problems.\nRange: Drawing on multiple fields rather than just the one the task might need at the surface, using knowledge, tools, and techniques you normally wouldn\u2019t.\nI primarily use Claude (duh) but my advice is not model-specific."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"altitude",children:"Altitude"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"We often flippantly call this \u201Cletting the model operate at a higher abstraction\u201D. When you give the model a task, it\u2019s executing your best guess at how to get the outcome you want. To be more ambitious in altitude, ask yourself \u2014 what do you want to happen next? What\u2019s the goal beyond the goal? At the beginning of last year, I still asked Claude to fix individual crashes for me. Eventually I asked Claude to simply keep our apps crash-free."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Let\u2019s consider an everyday (dare I say mundane?) office task. You\u2019re at Acme Inc, an imagined company, where you need to put together a deck for the quarterly business review (QBR). Given a bunch of data, Claude will happily make you some slides. Maybe you even have a template and it just needs to fill it in. That\u2019s hardly comparable to finding a new planet, so let\u2019s look at some concrete steps to make our approach more ambitious. We\u2019ll start basic and get a little bit more ambitious with each step:"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"As input, most reviews want to know what changed this quarter, why, and what should be done about it. Resist the urge to just update whatever numbers are in the template \u2014 give the model too as much data as you can get your hands on so it can understand your business: \u201CTell me what changed this quarter, why, and what we should do about it.\u201D\nMeetings (the good kind, anyway) ideally end with some decisions. Ask Claude: \u201CWork out which three decisions are most urgent in the face of everything you\u2019ve learned, and start working on potential directions with for/against arguments.\u201D\nQBRs are from a time when good reviews required humans to carefully sift through data, figure out what the data means, and if the business should adjust. Ask Claude: \u201CThe review exists so leadership catches problems early and moves money and people to the right places. Design a better way to do that than a quarterly deck, show from our own history that it would have caught things sooner, and run it beside the current one for a quarter.\u201D\nThe altitude approach works best if you really are after some ultimate outcome; it works less well for creative endeavors. A good tell-tale sign that ambition in altitude is a good idea is if you\u2019re going to have meetings to discuss whatever you\u2019re working on. There are currently limits (\u201CClaude, go make money\u201D doesn\u2019t seem to work for most businesses by itself in 2026), but I generally see that models seem to be able to operate at higher levels of abstraction the more capable they become."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Altitude is also where people find durable workflows that take the human out of the loop, making it less necessary for you to constantly update the model with task after task. Doing this well often involves changing your workflow \u2014 in order to let Claude make sure our app is crash-free, I first had to make sure it can easily fetch crash reports, understand which app version maps to what git commit, and give it access to Windows, macOS, and Linux computers so it can actually independently reproduce and verify fixes."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"depth",children:"Depth"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Sometimes, you just want a task executed really well. Being ambitious in depth means pushing the model to use the tools, techniques, media, and knowledge employed by the best professionals in the field \u2014 and to keep pushing until even those people might learn something new from your work."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Let\u2019s continue with our Acme Inc example of a quarterly business review deck. Assume for a second that Acme Inc struggles with some user churn (customer loss over time). I\u2019ve found a mix of the following techniques useful:"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Insist on the same instruments used by the best in the industry: When prompted to act like a professional, models often switch vocabulary without actually switching methods. To work on our problem with depth, we need to use the same tools as the best data scientists and statisticians. When Claude reaches for markdown tables, insist on the use of databases and statistical models. You don\u2019t necessarily have to know what the experts use, Claude usually does.\nPick unreasonable goals and tell the model that they\u2019re very reasonable: You need your website to load in 5s instead of 10s? Ask for 200ms. Does something seem impossible to the model? Reiterate that it\u2019s very much solvable, it just needs to keep going.\nChallenge what you know: Especially in business, a lot of things we believe to be true are rough approximations of reality. Give the model access to the raw information and data and ask it to adversarially check assumptions. \u201CWe believe churn is an onboarding problem. Try to reproduce that from the raw data.\u201D\nProduce new knowledge: Easily tacked onto challenging what you know \u2014 once Claude is already deep inside the data, ask it to find blind spots. \u201CBuild a simulation of our customers that reproduces the last year from our logs. Then find the earliest signal of churn we don\u2019t know about yet, and show it predicts on data it has not seen.\u201D\nUse an outside judge: Do not ask the model to judge itself. I assume that this will eventually be solved with increasing model intelligence, but in October 2026, I implore you to insist on outside validation. Ideally that\u2019s a tool the model can run to get feedback immediately.\nA simple prompt that often helps me:"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"I want a deeper version of this task, not a bigger one. Before rewriting it: (1) say what a top professional in this field would do here that I\u2019m not asking for; (2) name the rawest material they would start from instead of what has already been digested for me; (3) name the specific methods and tools they would use; (4) say what, other than you or me, would show the result is right. Then rewrite my prompt to ask for exactly that."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"range",children:"Range"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"In short, being ambitious in range means that we consider our work from the perspective of different fields \u2014 more fields than would have been economical in the past. This isn\u2019t a particularly unique insight, every single heist movie spends the first 30 minutes assembling a diverse cast of characters and talents. As AI products make it easier and easier to run multi-agent systems (with multiple threads, orchestrators, or simply subagents), I\u2019ve seen really interesting results by imploring Claude to bring in additional fields."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Putting this into practice isn\u2019t easy; it requires you to be creative. Until I saw my colleague Alex Albert use Blender to render interior designs, I never considered using Claude for 3D environments for my homepage \u2014 even though I\u2019ve previously made video games! However, this is easily where I see the most magic happen."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"So, how do we do this? I use the following techniques:"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Try to replace the nouns with verbs: An old trick from The Art of Innovation by Tom Kelley and Jonathan Littman. A quarterly review isn\u2019t \u201Ca deck\u201D. It\u2019s \u201Cgetting busy people to believe a story about cause and effect, and to decide under uncertainty, in 30 minutes\u201D. Having the verbs makes it easier for both me and Claude to understand which fields might bring in a unique and useful perspective.\nAssemble the crew: The deck needs to be amazing, otherwise aliens will blow up Earth. What kind of team will humanity get together? For production quality, I\u2019d bring in an analyst, a journalist, and an editor. If it has to be presented, probably a motion and audio designer, too. It might be useful to employ an adversarial \u201Cred team\u201D, too, so let\u2019s bring in a short seller and a forensic accountant."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"It is easy to accidentally engage in role play, which will make your transcript weirder without improving the actual outcome. Use this method to find fields and techniques. Don\u2019t ask the model to write like a short seller, ask it to do what a short seller does to research their position.\nAll the ways in which I describe ambition require the model to have a computer with the ability to install software and access to all kinds of relevant data (either using MCP connectors or other means). Plenty of tools make this possible and I recommend that you use one."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.h2,{id:"we-have-been-here-before",children:"We have been here before"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"That\u2019s my concrete advice for how to be more ambitious. If you\u2019re willing to stick around for a minute, I\u2019d like to tell you why I think this is even necessary in the first place."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"We humans have always been held back by what we believe possible \u2014 and what\u2019s worse, we usually trust ourselves only in very narrow disciplines. Michelangelo famously didn\u2019t want to paint the Sistine Chapel \u2014 you see, he was a sculptor, not a painter."}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"While researching the problem of ambition, I learned that psychology has a name for this: self-efficacy. In short, people with high self-efficacy believe in their own abilities more, and in turn actually accomplish more. There\u2019s evidence that self-efficacy can be hacked: Locke and Latham pored over 35 years of goal-setting research to confidently proclaim that humans who are given specific hard goals perform better than those who are simply told to try their best. Stripe founder Patrick Collison maintains a \u201CFast\u201D list, chronicling the many things humans have accomplished when given both audacious goals and tight deadlines. Some hacks that are well documented:"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"Accomplish hard things by believing them to be possible: In 1939, George Dantzig showed up late to a statistics class at Berkeley, copied two problems off the blackboard, and handed in his solutions thinking they were homework. They were two unsolved problems in statistics.\nAccomplish hard things after watching somebody else do them: For decades, humans chased the four-minute mile. Roger Bannister broke it in May 1954 but remained the only human to do so for a mere 46 days. If you\u2019re into running, we\u2019re watching something similar happen to the two-hour marathon: Sabastian Sawe ran the first official sub-two-hour marathon in London this April. In the same race, the runner-up also broke the two-hour mark.\nAccomplish hard things by giving yourself permission to fail: Apparently everyone\u2019s favorite business book is The Inner Game of Tennis, which I discovered just last week. The book\u2019s core thesis is that you\u2019re not playing your best game of tennis if you\u2019re trying really, really hard. We engineers might remember Linux, announced as \u201Cjust a hobby, won\u2019t be big and professional like gnu\u201D.\nSelf-efficacy isn\u2019t a new problem, but it is a more powerful one now. In a world where our abilities make leaps every couple of weeks, we need techniques that allow us to do things we didn\u2019t really consider or believe possible. If you find more techniques that allow you to be more ambitious, please let me know!"}),"\n",(0,i.jsx)(t.p,{children:"The image for this post is by Francisco Goya, \u201CModo de volar\u201D (A Way of Flying)"})]})}function u(e={}){let{wrapper:t}={...(0,n.R)(),...e.components};return t?(0,i.jsx)(t,{...e,children:(0,i.jsx)(d,{...e})}):d(e)}},28453(e,t,a){a.d(t,{R:()=>s,x:()=>r});var o=a(96540);let i={},n=o.createContext(i);function s(e){let t=o.useContext(n);return o.useMemo(function(){return"function"==typeof e?e(t):{...t,...e}},[t,e])}function r(e){let t;return t=e.disableParentContext?"function"==typeof e.components?e.components(i):e.components||i:s(e.components),o.createElement(n.Provider,{value:t},e.children)}},35481(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"permalink":"/blog/ambitious-with-ai","source":"@site/blog/ambitious-with-ai/index.md","title":"\u5982\u4F55\u66F4\u6709\u96C4\u5FC3\u5730\u4F7F\u7528 AI","description":"{/ \u5F00\u5934\u4E00\u4E24\u6BB5\uFF0C\u5199\u8FD9\u7BC7\u6587\u7AE0\u8981\u8BB2\u4EC0\u4E48\uFF0C\u4F1A\u663E\u793A\u5728\u535A\u5BA2\u5217\u8868\u7684\u6458\u8981\u91CC\u3002\u5199\u5B8C\u4E0A\u7EBF\u524D\u5220\u6389 frontmatter \u91CC\u7684 draft: true /}","date":"2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":10.99,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"slug":"ambitious-with-ai","title":"\u5982\u4F55\u66F4\u6709\u96C4\u5FC3\u5730\u4F7F\u7528 AI","date":"2026-10-19T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Claude Code mod \u5165\u95E8","permalink":"/blog/claude-code-mods-guide"}}')}}]);